A||||7||Cliff Pennock||How bad is it? (transmission problem)
Z||000000||Cliff Pennock||07-01-99||03:29 PM||||Today, after about a one hour drive and when I wanted to pull away from a traffic light, I noticed my V6 Automatic hardly shifted anymore. If I pulled up very slowly, it would get in gear but if I tried to accelerate more, it would go into neutral (or what looked like it). I was almost home so I drove it very carefully. After I had parked it, I put in neutral and drive a few times to see if the transmission was totally gone. Fortunately it wasn't (whenever I put it in 'D', I would feel it get into gear). I checked the transmission fluid and there was only a drop at the very bottom of the stick. Then I noticed the 'oil-track' on the road. I could see exactly where I had driven the car (I could see it all the way to the end of the street).<p>I think (hope) it's something as simple as a broken hose and I don't think the tranny has been damaged. If it was damaged, it wouldn't shift at all, would it? Or am I wrong? Could there still be something seriously damaged?
Z||000001||batboy||07-01-99||08:30 PM||||It all depends on how far you drove it while it was low on fluid. It's possible that if you took it real easy and didn't go too far, that it might be ok. But then again, maybe not. If I were you, I'd find out why and where it was leaking, fix that problem, add more fluid, cross your fingers, and hope for the best. Who knows, you might luck out. I ran a tranny low on fluid once when the cooling line that goes to the radiator sprung a leak. After replacing that line and filling it back up with fluid, it still worked all right. I hope it works out for you.
Z||000002||theogre||07-01-99||10:02 PM||||I agree. Fix what ever is leaking, and fill it up. The abloslute worse is that the car still won't go.<p>Automatic transmissions I've dealt with tend to be pretty forgiving of low fluid. They simply drop out of gear(or won't go into gear) and don't go. They need the oil as much to change/hold gears as they do for lube and cooling.<p>If it's a rubber line to the cooling system REPLACE IT AND THE CLAMPS. The line is available at any auto parts store in the world that's worth a shit.(Look in the area that they stock tranny coolers on the sales floor. it's not behind the counter at many stores in the states) Any slight diferance between metric, and US, dimesional line is not relevant. USE ONLY softline material rated for this purpose, Not vacuum line, Not Fuel line, etc. (I know Cliff has sence enough to do that. :) ) 
Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||07-01-99||10:23 PM||||My mom had an 84 Trans Am and once it went really low on tranny fluid. It would take 2 or 3 attempts to finally get into the next gear.I'm not sure how long she let it go like this,but the mechanic said usually automatic trannys will let you run them dry ONCE and be OK. He was right. I filled it up and it was fine. I'm crossing my fingers for you.....
Z||000004||lowCG||07-01-99||11:16 PM||||Reminds me of a time when my engine  oil drain plug dropped from the car,luckily,just as I was arriving to my garage.<br>Walked down the road,found the thing,put it back in,real tight!<br>Unless you blew a seal inside the bellhousing(very doubtful),it sounds like you'll be able to make it without too much hassle,and with no long term damage.<p>
Z||000005||Cliff Pennock||07-02-99||12:38 AM||||Thanks for the replies guys - I feel better now! :)<p>I've got a mechanic coming in an hour or so. Not that I can't make it myself, but I don't have a garage to put it in, and the car is out in the street. I'll let you know.
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||07-02-99||04:27 AM||||Phew! It turned out to be one of the hoses under the car that got loose. He clipped of a small piece and re-attached it. The hose still needs replacement because it was a bit worn but as far as the guy could tell, the tranny had not been damaged.<p>So fortunately this is one repair that will not cost me an arm and a leg! But I admit, I was a bit worried though...<p>I can't wait till my garage is finished...
Z||000007||four50four||07-16-99||04:28 PM||||well I may not know much about other trannys but i have changed and delt with numerous turbo th350 trannys from chevrolet trucks. what happens when they get low on fluid is they "slip" when you hit the gas at almost anytime as well if its low when pulling a load it slips. when it "slips" it feels like to goes into neutral and the engine just revs until the hooks up again. snowplowing does this to the mighty TH350 so i am acustommed to changing them. the amzing part is that GM's turbo 350s and turbo 400s are incredibly durible for the stress they take. they can be run out of fluid many times and still run, not that its good for them. had a problem with a NP205 transfer case leaking and i don't know if you guys know anything about these but it uses tranny fluid through the tranny so with this leak, was constantly running out. any way i have no idea about fiero automatics but since they are GM products i think they are somewhat similar. just a thought.
